  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nEarly this morning, upper air analysis shows a weak mid/upper cutoff \nlow spinning over northern Texas with a mid/upper ridge axis \nextending from southern California to the central plains. Meanwhile, \na sharp deep layer trough is quickly progressing southeastward \nacross the PNW and northern Rockies. At the surface, a very weak\nfrontal boundary is drifting southward across Kansas with very \nlight winds on either side of this boundary. Despite these calm \nwinds and clear skies leading to optimal radiational cooling, \ndewpoint depressions remain just a tick too high for the \ndevelopment of fog across the area. That being said, short term \nguidance suggests a brief period of patchy fog is possible \naround sunrise before quickly mixing out by mid-morning. \nContinued weak low-level CAA on the north side of the frontal \nboundary will keep afternoon highs in the mid 60s across central\nKansas. Meanwhile, locations across southern and southeast \nKansas should see afternoon highs in the 70s. With the frontal \nboundary washing out later today, a seasonably mild airmass \nwill continue to linger keeping overnight lows in the 40s and \n50s.\n\nThe aforementioned trough over the PNW will continue rapidly making \nits way southeastward throughout the day today, and the trough \naxis will likely be located over the central plains by this time\nWednesday morning. In response, lee cyclogenesis is forecast \nacross the northern High Plains later this afternoon, and by \nWednesday morning, the surface cyclone will be located over the \nUpper Midwest with an associated cold front pushing southward \nacross Kansas. In the cold front's wake, a surface ridge is set \nto dominate the region through the end of the work week ushering\nin a cooler airmass through Friday. While highs Wednesday \nafternoon are still forecast to reach the 60s, Thursday and \nFriday's highs will likely only be in the 50s which is just a \ntick below average for this time of year. In fact, temperatures \nare likely to flirt with the freezing mark both Thursday and \nFriday nights. Heading into the weekend, guidance continues to \nshow a brief period of mid/upper ridging residing over the \ncentral plains before a vigorous system arrives late in the \nweekend or early next week. As a result, Sunday now features \nrain chances across the forecast area, but potential amounts \nappear low at this time. Looking beyond the forecast period, \nlong range global guidance appears to be confident in increased \nprobabilities for above average precipitation for next week, but\nwe're still too far out for exact details at this time.",
